---
import { PLATE_LABEL, STUDIO_EMAIL } from '../lib/copy';

/**
 * DESIGN.md §B.10 — `position: sticky; bottom: 0` on an element inside a wrapper
 * spanning from the end of the hero to the start of the final CTA. It appears
 * when the hero leaves, follows down the page, and retires on its own when the
 * real CTA arrives. No JS, no scroll listener, no dismiss state.
 *
 * QUESTIONS.md item 53: the plate prints the address itself, not a label.
 *
 * §B.10's address table is per world, and QUESTIONS.md item 66 confirmed it: the studio
 * pages carry `thegeekdogs@gmail.com`, `/sahib/` carries his and `/tanya/` hers. The
 * plate and the page never disagree about who is being written to, which is the whole
 * reason the person pages get their own addresses at all. The component does not change
 * width between them: set at 14px Instrument 600 with 20px of padding each side, the
 * longest of the three needs 209px of the 260 available (§B.10).
 *
 * COPY.md §1 gives one accessible-name pattern for all three, with the page's own
 * address substituted, so the spoken name and the printed label never name different
 * people.
 *
 * **Below 768 the plate is 112 x 56 and prints a LABEL rather than the address.**
 * §B.10 round 10 withdrew the 260px address plate at that width on measured evidence —
 * at 360 it covered 236 of the card panel's 280px measure, 84% of the line — because 112
 * with its padding leaves ~73px of type against the 169px the shortest address needs.
 * Both strings were `[COPY NEEDED]` through run B and the element was held; COPY.md §1
 * now carries them, so the plate renders at every width.
 *
 * - Visible label below 768: `Email`. One string for all three worlds; it carries no
 *   pronoun on purpose, because `us` would name the studio while the `mailto:` names one
 *   person, and §B.10 requires that the plate and the page never disagree about who is
 *   being written to.
 * - Accessible name, one pattern at BOTH widths: `Email <address>. Opens a new message
 *   about a project.` with the page's own address substituted. It is unchanged from the
 *   >= 768 plate on purpose — below 768 it is the only place the address is spoken, so
 *   the pattern does not shorten with the plate.
 *
 * Both visible forms are contained in that one name — `Email` is its first word and the
 * address is its second — so SC 2.5.3, Label in Name, holds at both widths without a
 * second string.
 *
 * Item 53 is amended below 768, not overturned: the address still prints in plain text
 * in the page's own final CTA, three times on /contact/, and in each person page's
 * closing block. What the plate gives up at that width is being THE printed proof; what
 * it keeps is being the way to act.
 */
interface Props {
  address?: string;
}

const { address = STUDIO_EMAIL } = Astro.props;
---

<a
  class="plate"
  data-cta="contact-plate"
  href={`mailto:${address}`}
  aria-label={`Email ${address}. Opens a new message about a project.`}
  ><span class="plate__label">{PLATE_LABEL}</span><span class="plate__address">{address}</span></a
>

<style>
  /*
    §B.10 round 10 — two widths, ONE object. Nothing but which of the two strings is
    rendered changes here: the fill, the border, the radius, the type size, the inset,
    the focus ring and the print rule are all on `.plate` in global.css and are shared.
    The width itself is `--plate-width`, which tokens.css already switches at 768.

    Exactly one of the two spans is in the box at any width, so the visible label is
    never both and never neither.
  */
  .plate__label {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .plate__label {
      display: inline;
    }

    .plate__address {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
